The returning beams recombine and enter a telescope, producing an interference pattern of alternating bright and dark fringes (Figure 217).
The fringe positions depend on the difference between the times taken along the two light paths;
a change in that time difference changes the fringe positions.

When the apparatus was rotated, the researchers did not detect the fringe shift predicted by the model within the experiment's sensitivity;
this is a "null result", not a measurement showing that every change is exactly zero.

The source also describes repeated experiments at different times of day and seasons of the year, which likewise did not detect the predicted motion of Earth relative to the ether.
Its stated sensitivity to changes in light speed of 2 metres per second refers to later experiments, not the precision of the first experiment;
it does not turn a finite-precision result into an infinitely precise measurement.

The source compares the surprise to imagining a car travelling at 100 kilometres per hour with no oncoming airflow felt outside it.
The analogy highlights the contrast between expectation and observation;
it is a description to imagine, not an activity to try in a moving car.

The lack of an observed predicted fringe shift did not support the specific stationary luminiferous-ether model being tested, or the proposal to treat that ether's rest frame as a preferred frame.
However, a null result at finite sensitivity cannot strictly prove that every possible preferred frame or special medium is impossible;
the conclusion is limited to the tested model's predictions and the experiment's detection range.
